{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791315677,"nonce":"SL8aXV0+wuGnpODbkRa5tnn0SfY=","title":"HIR: eliminate impossible integer tag comparisons using known bits","message":"The Cranelift-only NUC matrix at 57b6a9167470ed4cbc796d4e2ba40282a1a12e7b shows Lua nbody kernel 171.920 ms vs LLVM 114.303 ms. Scalar select CSE and tag encode/decode cleanup remove 80 lines from advance (109 -\u003e 91 selects, 89 -\u003e 33 equality checks), but an alternating comparison still runs advance at 168.210 vs 168.336 ms. Cranelift handles much of that local redundancy already.\n\nThe remaining HIR has numeric tags 3 for integer and 4 for float, followed by patterns such as (tag | 4) == 3. This is provably false for every tag, but HIR keeps the comparison and the integer/float selection chain. Float-involving arithmetic then still carries integer intermediates until native optimization. LLVM has known-bits reasoning; the shared HIR constant folder currently only evaluates fully constant binary operands and a few scalar identities.\n\nImplement bounded width-aware integer known-bits reasoning in the shared HIR optimizer, starting with contradictory Eq/Ne checks on AND/OR masks. Preserve integer widths and signedness, arbitrary dynamic values, side effects of operand producers, and exact floating-point behavior; leave unknown cases generic. Validate exhaustive small-width cases and live interpreter/Cranelift/LLVM parity, inspect actual advance HIR/CLIF, and require NUC execution/wall evidence. Follow-on work can extend to select arms or loop guards once bounded transfer rules are proven.\n\nArtifacts: /tmp/zyntax-cl-only-nbody-hir and /tmp/zyntax-hir-selects-nbody-hir on Mac; /tmp/zyntax-nuc-hir-selects-lua.json on Mac/NUC. Related scalar-select cleanup c1567c94053107e81cebb37e0ce19c773c481342e475836a912bebf5b1a5e8f1.","files":null}]}